# Common React Project Structure and Conventions

This project uses React for building the user interface. Here are some common conventions and structure guidelines to help you navigate and understand the codebase:

## Main Entry Point
- The main entry point is typically `src/App.jsx`, which sets up the main application routes and layout.
- Global styles and third-party CSS (like Bootstrap) are imported in `App.jsx` or `index.js`.

## Components
- Reusable UI elements are placed in the `src/components/` directory.
- Components are usually functional components, using hooks for state and side effects.
- Component files are named with PascalCase (e.g., `Header.jsx`, `SearchModal.jsx`).
- Related CSS files are often placed alongside the component (e.g., `Header.jsx` and `Header.css`).

## Pages
- Route-level components (pages) are placed in `src/pages/`.
- Each page may have its own directory (e.g., `src/pages/Category/Category.jsx`).
- Pages are rendered via React Router in `App.jsx`.

## Routing
- Routing is handled with `react-router-dom`.
- The main `<Routes>` and `<Route>` setup is in `App.jsx`.
- Nested routes are used for layouts (e.g., `UserLayout`, `AdminLayout`).

## State Management
- Local state is managed with React's `useState` and `useEffect` hooks.
- For global state, context or external libraries may be used (not present in this starter).

## Best Practices
- Use functional components and hooks.
- Keep components small and focused.
- Use prop-types or TypeScript for type safety (if configured).
- Keep styles modular and scoped to components when possible.
- Use clear, descriptive names for components and props.

## Example File References
- [App.jsx](mdc:src/App.jsx): Main app and routing
- [Header.jsx](mdc:src/components/Header/Header.jsx): Example of a reusable header component
- [Category.jsx](mdc:src/pages/Category/Category.jsx): Example of a page component

Refer to these conventions when adding new components, pages, or features to the project.

